    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $f:\\mathbb{Z}\\to\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$ where $f(n)=|n|$. Show that $f$ is surjective, but not injective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is surjective because every non-negative integer $m$ satisfies $f(m)=m$. It is not injective because $f(1)=f(-1)=1$.",
          "worked": "Let $m$ be any non-negative integer. Since $m\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ and $f(m)=|m|=m$, every element of the codomain has a preimage. Hence, $f$ is surjective.\n\nHowever, $f(1)=1=f(-1)$ and $1\\ne-1$. Hence, $f$ is not injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $f:\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{Z}$ where $f(n)=-n$. Show that $f$ is injective, but not surjective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is injective because $f(m)=f(n)$ gives $m=n$. It is not surjective because $1$ has no preimage.",
          "worked": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and $f(m)=f(n)$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}-m&=-n\\\\m&=n.\\end{align*}$$\nHence, $f$ is injective.\n\nThe integer $1$ is not in the range: $f(n)=1$ would require $n=-1\\notin\\mathbb{N}$. Hence, $f$ is not surjective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      }
    ]
  },
